WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:02.102 --> 00:00:03.236
Function calling is

2
00:00:03.236 --> 00:00:06.306
one of the most exciting developments
by mobile providers.

3
00:00:06.306 --> 00:00:08.975
And what if you've got
a number of pipelines like react

4
00:00:08.975 --> 00:00:12.078
pipelines,
that you want to use as functions

5
00:00:12.078 --> 00:00:15.682
in this lesson we will use open
AI function called and capability

6
00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:20.353
to create a chat agent
that can use haystack pipelines as tools.

7
00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:21.888
Let's see how this goes.

8
00:00:21.888 --> 00:00:26.693
Let's build our first chat agent
that can use function calling.

9
00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:30.296
In this lesson,
we're going to be building with components

10
00:00:30.296 --> 00:00:31.898
that we call chat generators.

11
00:00:31.898 --> 00:00:34.434
And we're going to build chat agents
with them.

12
00:00:34.434 --> 00:00:37.270
With these components
we work with messages.

13
00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:41.708
And messages can come from user
agent system or function.

14
00:00:41.708 --> 00:00:44.811
We can also provide these generators
with tools.

15
00:00:44.811 --> 00:00:48.281
For example
the user ask where does Mark live?

16
00:00:48.281 --> 00:00:52.185
And the generator simply responds with
I don't know, but

17
00:00:52.185 --> 00:00:56.990
the generator can be provided with tools
that can help it through this process.

18
00:00:56.990 --> 00:01:00.060
These tools can be anything from haystack

19
00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:03.396
pipelines to different APIs, etc..

20
00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:05.031
Let's start building our own.

21
00:01:05.031 --> 00:01:08.401
Let's start with the usual
warning suppression, and make sure

22
00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:12.472
we load all of the environment variables
we'll be using in this lesson.

23
00:01:12.472 --> 00:01:17.010
Next,
let's import all of our dependencies.

24
00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:18.812
Now we can start by creating

25
00:01:18.812 --> 00:01:22.582
a pipeline
and providing this pipeline as a function.

26
00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:27.387
For this first bit we're going to be
creating our usual react pipeline.

27
00:01:27.387 --> 00:01:30.123
And we're going to be using the default
OpenAI

28
00:01:30.123 --> 00:01:33.626
generator model,
which is going to be GPT three.

29
00:01:33.626 --> 00:01:36.162
By now
you'll use these types of pipelines.

30
00:01:36.162 --> 00:01:39.399
But what's happening
here, in short, is was simply asking

31
00:01:39.399 --> 00:01:42.569
for the large language model
to respond to a query.

32
00:01:42.569 --> 00:01:47.107
Now we're going to also be wrapping
this pipeline in a function.

33
00:01:47.107 --> 00:01:49.242
Now that we have this pipeline.

34
00:01:49.242 --> 00:01:54.242
Let's define a normal Python function
and let's call it pipeline func.

35
00:01:54.848 --> 00:01:56.516
And it's expecting query.

36
00:01:56.516 --> 00:01:59.953
In this pipeline
we're going to be taking a shortcut.

37
00:01:59.953 --> 00:02:02.489
And we're going to be providing
dummy data.

38
00:02:02.489 --> 00:02:05.158
So we know that the documents
that this pipeline

39
00:02:05.158 --> 00:02:08.628
is going to be generating response
from are these documents.

40
00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:12.499
As you can see, these are all documents
about who lives where.

41
00:02:12.499 --> 00:02:15.468
For example, Mark lives in Dublin,
Georgia, lives in Rome.

42
00:02:15.468 --> 00:02:16.669
And so on.

43
00:02:16.669 --> 00:02:21.007
when this function has an input,
we want to run our pipeline

44
00:02:21.007 --> 00:02:25.211
and we want to provide documents
to our prompt builder

45
00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:28.047
based on the documents we created above.

46
00:02:28.047 --> 00:02:32.619
We also want to provide the query
as a question to our prompt builder.

47
00:02:32.619 --> 00:02:34.854
This function is then going to return

48
00:02:34.854 --> 00:02:39.359
the results of the pipeline in reply.

49
00:02:39.359 --> 00:02:40.493
Now we have

50
00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:44.330
our Hastag pipeline
provided as a function.

51
00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:47.333
Next you're going to be creating
a weather function.

52
00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:50.103
This time you're going to be simulating
a weather function.

53
00:02:50.103 --> 00:02:51.204
But you can imagine that

54
00:02:51.204 --> 00:02:56.204
this actually accesses real time
live weather data from a different API.

55
00:02:56.376 --> 00:02:59.946
In this case, again
we're going to be providing dummy data.

56
00:02:59.946 --> 00:03:02.916
you'll provide information
about what the weather

57
00:03:02.916 --> 00:03:06.286
is like in Berlin,
Paris, Rome, Madrid and London.

58
00:03:06.286 --> 00:03:08.221
As you can see, we've made these up.

59
00:03:08.221 --> 00:03:12.158
It's mostly sunny,
mostly cloudy, sunny or cloudy and so on.

60
00:03:12.158 --> 00:03:15.094
This is also the same example
that OpenAI provides

61
00:03:15.094 --> 00:03:18.398
in the documentation to explain function
calling.

62
00:03:18.398 --> 00:03:22.101
Let's now provide this
in a get current weather function.

63
00:03:22.101 --> 00:03:26.472
Again we're going to have a function
that's expecting location this time.

64
00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:29.075
And it's going to return weather data.

65
00:03:29.075 --> 00:03:32.078
We'll first look up
whether the location is present

66
00:03:32.078 --> 00:03:35.982
in our weather info, but
if not, we'll also have a fallback here.

67
00:03:35.982 --> 00:03:38.218
We're just going to be making up something

68
00:03:38.218 --> 00:03:42.355
if we don't have the city
listed in our weather info,

69
00:03:42.355 --> 00:03:47.355
we'll simply be returning sunny
with a temperature of 21.8°F.

70
00:03:48.228 --> 00:03:49.829
Now you have two functions.

71
00:03:49.829 --> 00:03:53.399
One of them is a haystack Rock pipeline
that's able to respond

72
00:03:53.399 --> 00:03:57.937
to where people live,
and the other is a weather function.

73
00:03:57.937 --> 00:04:00.406
Now that we have our functions ready,

74
00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:03.576
we have to provide them
as tools to a generator.

75
00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:06.112
In this use case,
we're going to be using OpenAI.

76
00:04:06.112 --> 00:04:11.112
So we have to describe our tools
in a way that the OpenAI API expects.

77
00:04:11.184 --> 00:04:14.754
So let's have a look
at how that might look like.

78
00:04:14.754 --> 00:04:18.024
This is going to be a lot of data,
but let's walk through it.

79
00:04:18.024 --> 00:04:20.493
Tools are going to be a list of tools.

80
00:04:20.493 --> 00:04:23.263
And what
providing the name of our functions,

81
00:04:23.263 --> 00:04:26.733
as well as a description
that the language model can use to decide

82
00:04:26.733 --> 00:04:29.369
whether this is the function
that it should use or not.

83
00:04:29.369 --> 00:04:33.172
Each tool also describes
what kind of inputs they're expecting.

84
00:04:33.172 --> 00:04:36.776
In this case,
our pipeline func is expecting query,

85
00:04:36.776 --> 00:04:40.346
and we're also providing a description
of what that query is.

86
00:04:40.346 --> 00:04:42.982
For example,
the query to use in the search.

87
00:04:42.982 --> 00:04:47.982
We also tell it to infer this
from the user's message and so on.

88
00:04:48.221 --> 00:04:50.456
We do the same for get weather.

89
00:04:50.456 --> 00:04:54.527
We describe the get weather function
as getting the current weather,

90
00:04:54.527 --> 00:04:58.998
and we also describe
how it's expecting location.

91
00:04:58.998 --> 00:05:00.867
Now we have a list of tools.

92
00:05:00.867 --> 00:05:05.867
We can provide these list of tools
to what we call an OpenAI chat generator.

93
00:05:06.039 --> 00:05:08.608
Let's have a look at that.

94
00:05:08.608 --> 00:05:12.712
Let's start
by creating our AI chat generator.

95
00:05:12.712 --> 00:05:16.816
This component
not only is able to work with model names,

96
00:05:16.816 --> 00:05:20.820
but we can also provide tools
in generation Kwargs.

97
00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:25.358
So we'll going to be creating a generator
which we're calling the chat generator.

98
00:05:25.358 --> 00:05:28.361
And we're going to be providing our tools
as tools.

99
00:05:28.661 --> 00:05:33.661
Next let's see what happens
when we run this component with messages.

100
00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:38.104
This component is special
in that it expects a list of messages.

101
00:05:38.104 --> 00:05:42.608
And we can also describe
where the message is coming from.

102
00:05:42.608 --> 00:05:43.743
In this case, we're going

103
00:05:43.743 --> 00:05:47.547
to be providing a chat message
from a user.

104
00:05:47.547 --> 00:05:51.117
And the question is where does Mark live?

105
00:05:51.117 --> 00:05:54.520
Well, assigning the responses
from the NLM to replies.

106
00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:57.223
Let's see what replies contains.

107
00:05:57.223 --> 00:06:00.226
As you can see,
this again is a chat message,

108
00:06:00.226 --> 00:06:03.896
and you can notice that the chat message
is coming from an assistant

109
00:06:03.896 --> 00:06:07.600
and that it's saying that
it should run a function with the query.

110
00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:11.137
Where does Mark live
and the name of that function is right?

111
00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:13.005
Pipeline func.

112
00:06:13.005 --> 00:06:15.942
The important thing to notice
here is the response that you're

113
00:06:15.942 --> 00:06:19.812
getting from these models
are not the function's results.

114
00:06:19.812 --> 00:06:22.715
it's more about which function
to run and how.

115
00:06:22.715 --> 00:06:27.715
Next, let's see what happens
if we want to call these functions.

116
00:06:28.087 --> 00:06:31.991
For this we're going to be using
a components called the open AI function

117
00:06:31.991 --> 00:06:36.629
caller which is actually coming
from the Hastag experimental package.

118
00:06:36.629 --> 00:06:41.367
This component expects available functions
when being initialized,

119
00:06:41.367 --> 00:06:46.367
and it will run the functions
that our chat generator is asking to run,

120
00:06:46.406 --> 00:06:51.406
as well as add the responses from
those function runs to the message queue.

121
00:06:51.411 --> 00:06:54.180
So first let's initialize our function

122
00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:58.885
caller
and let's provide our available functions.

123
00:06:58.885 --> 00:07:02.588
Next, let's run this function caller

124
00:07:02.588 --> 00:07:05.758
with the messages that we had earlier.

125
00:07:05.758 --> 00:07:09.595
So our message queue
earlier contained the fact

126
00:07:09.595 --> 00:07:14.300
that we want to run RAC pipeline func with
where does Mark live?

127
00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:16.369
Let's assign that to results.

128
00:07:16.369 --> 00:07:21.369
And then let's print out these results
to see how this function called reacted.

129
00:07:21.507 --> 00:07:22.074
All right.

130
00:07:22.074 --> 00:07:26.579
In this case notice
we now have two messages in the queue.

131
00:07:26.579 --> 00:07:31.579
The first message was the assistant asking
for RAC pipeline func to be run with.

132
00:07:31.584 --> 00:07:33.586
Where does Mark live.

133
00:07:33.586 --> 00:07:37.323
The second
however is a response from function

134
00:07:37.323 --> 00:07:41.227
and the response from the function
is Marc lives in Berlin.

135
00:07:41.227 --> 00:07:45.164
reason is we've now
actually run RAC pipeline function

136
00:07:45.164 --> 00:07:48.234
and our RAC pipeline has responded
with one of the documents

137
00:07:48.234 --> 00:07:51.537
we have in our documents
that we provided to RAC.

138
00:07:51.537 --> 00:07:53.206
Marc lives in Berlin.

139
00:07:53.206 --> 00:07:56.742
Now that we have an OpenAI chat generator

140
00:07:56.742 --> 00:08:00.813
that's able to access tools
and also an open function caller,

141
00:08:00.813 --> 00:08:04.383
we can create a chat agent,
which is accessing tools.

142
00:08:04.383 --> 00:08:05.384
For this pipeline,

143
00:08:05.384 --> 00:08:09.188
we're going to be starting off
with a component we haven't used before.

144
00:08:09.188 --> 00:08:11.924
We're going to start with a branch joiner,

145
00:08:11.924 --> 00:08:16.924
which is going to be joining messages
from multiple components outputs into one.

146
00:08:17.463 --> 00:08:20.366
We'll see why we do that in a bit.

147
00:08:20.366 --> 00:08:20.967
Next we're

148
00:08:20.967 --> 00:08:24.103
going to be using our same chat generator.

149
00:08:24.103 --> 00:08:26.072
providing it with the tools.

150
00:08:26.072 --> 00:08:29.675
And finally we're going to be
using our function caller.

151
00:08:29.675 --> 00:08:34.013
Again we're providing our function caller
with the available functions we have.

152
00:08:34.013 --> 00:08:39.013
Now let's see how we add these components
to our pipeline and how we connect

153
00:08:39.719 --> 00:08:44.090
This time we're creating a pipeline
called the chat agent.

154
00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:47.527
And we're adding all of our components
to this chat agents.

155
00:08:47.527 --> 00:08:51.063
Finally, we're going to be connecting
the message collector outputs

156
00:08:51.063 --> 00:08:52.965
to generated messages.

157
00:08:52.965 --> 00:08:56.602
Next we're going to be asking
for the generated responses

158
00:08:56.602 --> 00:08:59.972
to go through the function
call to the function call.

159
00:08:59.972 --> 00:09:03.109
That will only call functions
if the assistant has asked

160
00:09:03.109 --> 00:09:04.443
for a function to be called.

161
00:09:04.443 --> 00:09:08.781
If not, it will simply be responding
with the messages that it has.

162
00:09:08.781 --> 00:09:09.715
Next.

163
00:09:09.715 --> 00:09:13.686
This is where we actually implement
another loop function call.

164
00:09:13.686 --> 00:09:17.290
The replies are going back to the message
collector.

165
00:09:17.290 --> 00:09:21.260
You can skip this if you like,
but I like to have this because this means

166
00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:23.729
that if there is a response
from a function,

167
00:09:23.729 --> 00:09:26.999
we are not simply providing the pair
response from the function.

168
00:09:26.999 --> 00:09:30.970
We're asking a large language model
to generate a human readable response

169
00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:32.238
from it. Again.

170
00:09:32.238 --> 00:09:33.606
Now we have this pipeline.

171
00:09:33.606 --> 00:09:35.875
Let's visualize it
and see what's going on.

172
00:09:35.875 --> 00:09:37.109
And we'll also have a look at

173
00:09:37.109 --> 00:09:41.881
why we're using this branch joiner
as our message collector.

174
00:09:41.881 --> 00:09:42.682
All right.

175
00:09:42.682 --> 00:09:45.785
So in this case
we have a message collector.

176
00:09:45.785 --> 00:09:49.155
And this is very useful
because the generator

177
00:09:49.155 --> 00:09:51.691
is expecting a list of messages.

178
00:09:51.691 --> 00:09:56.691
But we want those messages to either
come from the function caller or a user.

179
00:09:56.796 --> 00:10:01.534
However, this component
can only get inputs from one thing.

180
00:10:01.534 --> 00:10:06.534
So the message collector acts as a sort of
join a combiner of those messages.

181
00:10:07.139 --> 00:10:11.043
Messages can either come from the user now
or from function call.

182
00:10:11.043 --> 00:10:12.545
The replies.

183
00:10:12.545 --> 00:10:16.649
Now that we have our chat agents,
let's actually see it in action.

184
00:10:16.649 --> 00:10:20.853
Let's start our message
queue with a system message.

185
00:10:20.853 --> 00:10:22.321
This message states

186
00:10:22.321 --> 00:10:26.692
that if needed, break down users
questions into simpler questions.

187
00:10:26.692 --> 00:10:30.096
Don't make assumptions about what
the values to plug into functions are,

188
00:10:30.096 --> 00:10:31.998
and so on.

189
00:10:31.998 --> 00:10:34.967
Next, let's start a while loop and start

190
00:10:34.967 --> 00:10:38.104
asking for user inputs.

191
00:10:38.104 --> 00:10:42.875
This is where we start asking users
to start interacting with our chat agents.

192
00:10:42.875 --> 00:10:45.344
We'll also add a break statement here.

193
00:10:45.344 --> 00:10:50.344
So if you like you can type quit or exit
and you can exit the chat.

194
00:10:50.416 --> 00:10:55.416
Next we want to append the incoming
user messages to our message queue,

195
00:10:55.588 --> 00:11:00.588
and we want to get a response from
our chat agents based on that message.

196
00:11:01.193 --> 00:11:03.262
Again, we want to extend the messages

197
00:11:03.262 --> 00:11:07.833
with all of the responses
we may get from our chat agents.

198
00:11:07.833 --> 00:11:11.771
And finally, let's print out the response.

199
00:11:11.771 --> 00:11:12.338
All right.

200
00:11:12.338 --> 00:11:14.173
Here we start the chat.

201
00:11:14.173 --> 00:11:19.173
Let's start by asking
where does Mark live?

202
00:11:20.012 --> 00:11:23.482
Mark lives in Berlin
now because we have a message queue.

203
00:11:23.516 --> 00:11:26.218
We have a sort of primitive memory here.

204
00:11:26.218 --> 00:11:29.522
So we can ask questions in context,
and the yellow line

205
00:11:29.522 --> 00:11:31.791
should still be able to respond.

206
00:11:31.791 --> 00:11:36.791
So let's ask,
what's the weather like there?

207
00:11:38.764 --> 00:11:39.565
The current weather in

208
00:11:39.565 --> 00:11:42.702
Berlin is mostly sunny with a temperature
seven degrees Celsius.

209
00:11:42.735 --> 00:11:44.870
This is great because I happen to remember

210
00:11:44.870 --> 00:11:48.808
this is exactly what our get weather
function should be responding with.

211
00:11:48.808 --> 00:11:52.745
All right, so let's quit
this and see what else we can do.

212
00:11:52.745 --> 00:11:56.549
We can provide the same exact application
as a radio app.

213
00:11:56.549 --> 00:12:01.220
If you like let's start with the same
system message in our message queue.

214
00:12:01.220 --> 00:12:04.423
And then let's define a chat function.

215
00:12:04.457 --> 00:12:08.828
This is going to be used by radio
to create a chat interface.

216
00:12:08.828 --> 00:12:12.565
Next we provide the chat interface
as a demo.

217
00:12:12.598 --> 00:12:17.598
We can also provide some examples of what
we can ask this chat agent.

218
00:12:17.937 --> 00:12:21.974
And finally let's launch our demo.

219
00:12:21.974 --> 00:12:25.411
You should now also be seeing this type of interface, where you can start

220
00:12:25.411 --> 00:12:29.482
chatting with your agents
that has access to the tools you created.

221
00:12:29.482 --> 00:12:31.150
Let's start asking some questions.

222
00:12:31.150 --> 00:12:35.187
For example, where does Mark live?

223
00:12:35.187 --> 00:12:35.688
Great.

224
00:12:35.688 --> 00:12:39.058
Mark lives in Berlin
and we also know that that's accurate.

225
00:12:39.058 --> 00:12:44.058
Next let's ask
what's the weather like there?

226
00:12:45.965 --> 00:12:47.733
Notice how we don't have to specify

227
00:12:47.733 --> 00:12:51.203
Berlin because model has access
to a message queue.

228
00:12:51.203 --> 00:12:54.373
So it should be able to infer that
we're asking about Berlin.

229
00:12:54.373 --> 00:12:56.008
let's ask another follow up question.

230
00:12:56.008 --> 00:12:59.011
For example, what's the appropriate
clothing for him today?

231
00:12:59.011 --> 00:12:59.779
And there we are.

232
00:12:59.779 --> 00:13:00.713
We get an answer

233
00:13:00.713 --> 00:13:05.050
based on the current weather in Berlin
and what he could be wearing today.

234
00:13:05.050 --> 00:13:08.320
You can try playing around
with this chat app and maybe even provide

235
00:13:08.320 --> 00:13:09.555
some of your own tools.

236
00:13:09.555 --> 00:13:11.924
Here we simulated a weather function.

237
00:13:11.924 --> 00:13:14.994
For example, you can try and see
whether you can replace that

238
